{
  "schema_version": 1,
  "analysis": "ac",
  "purpose": "ngspice's VDMOS gate capacitances: CGS, CGDMIN, CGDMAX default to 1.4n, 20p and 2n when their keys are absent (vdmosset.c:70-80), Cgd follows devsup.c:654-665's atan/tanh law, and the instance m makes m parallel copies of both, as ngspice master does (2bc63e, 6bd5c2) where 44.2 and 45 leave them unscaled (vdmosload.c:71-74). ESPice gave the default card no gate capacitance and used a tanh-only law (5.04e-11 F for 9.87e-11 F at vgd = -7 V on card GIVEN).",
  "oracle": {
    "kind": "ngspice",
    "executable_sha256": "dc4f01e9939875529334c55c0a14f422843ad88e5c51422ae2640c4ab79a0b1f",
    "derivation": "ngspice 44.2 batch run of this deck with each m=2 instance (MD13, MG5, MS2, MF1, MF2) written as two m=1 instances on the same nodes, which is what m means in ngspice master (2bc63e \"VDMOS scaling fixed\", 6bd5c2) and what 44.2 computes correctly; values read from its ASCII raw, and ngspice 45 gives every one of these gate currents to the last bit on that netlist. Corroborated by the closed form: every imaginary part equals 2 pi 1e6 m Cgd(vgd), Cgd = s atan(A vgd) + y for vgd <= 0 and s tanh(A vgd) + y above, s = (CGDMAX - CGDMIN)/(1 + pi/2), y = CGDMAX - s, to 1e-15 relative, and i(vgs) = -2 pi 1e6 (CGS + Cgd(0)) = -j 16.52359 mA, i(vgs2) twice that; card FLAT's i(vgf0..2) are 2 pi 1e6 times 300p, 600p and -1.6n. The real parts are 0.",
    "command": "sed -E 's/^([^ *.]+)(.*) m=2$/\\1_1\\2\\n\\1_2\\2/' vdmos_gate_caps.sp > vdmos_gate_caps_x.sp && SPICE_ASCIIRAWFILE=1 ngspice -n -b -r ref.raw vdmos_gate_caps_x.sp",
    "version": "44.2",
    "tolerance": "rtol 1e-3, ngspice's reltol; the currents are linear in the capacitances at a pinned bias, so the oracle carries no Newton error. atol 1e-12 A."
  },
